# Welcome to the ClickHouse service

This service manages a ClickHouse database (columnar OLAP store):

- exposes a native TCP endpoint (port 9000)
- exports a `connection` string as configuration to dependent services
- applies SQL migrations (golang-migrate) on startup

It also supports the SHARED-database pattern: several services can target this
ONE ClickHouse instance while each owns its own `migrations/` folder, via the
`migration-sources` setting. Each source tracks its own version lineage in a
`schema_migrations_<name>` table, so per-service migration sequences never
collide.

## The engine in a cluster

The deployed engine is given what the clickhouse image reads to bootstrap
itself: `CLICKHOUSE_USER` and `CLICKHOUSE_PASSWORD` from the credentials this
service is configured with, and `CLICKHOUSE_DB` from its `database-name`. The
codefly carriers that deliver those values name a module and a service, which
clickhouse parses not at all, so without this the engine would come up on its
own built-in `default` user and never create this service's database. Where no
secret value may be rendered into the manifests, the credentials are the
references the environment's secret store declared for those same carriers,
aliased under the names the image reads; a deployment that declares neither is
refused by name.

The image creates the database only while its data directory is empty, so the
render also sets `CLICKHOUSE_ALWAYS_RUN_INITDB_SCRIPTS`, which makes that one
step (`CREATE DATABASE IF NOT EXISTS`) run on every start — the same thing the
nix runtime does on every Init. Nothing is mounted at
`/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d`, so that is all it re-runs.

## Migrations in a cluster

Under `codefly run` the agent applies the migrations itself, in-process, before
Init returns. A deployment instead renders a one-shot bootstrap Job that runs
the migration image built from `migrations/`.

That Job is given the address it migrates against: the deploy sets
`CODEFLY__SERVICE_CONFIGURATION__<MODULE>__<SERVICE>__CLICKHOUSE__ADDRESS` to
the in-cluster instance codefly handed this service's `tcp` endpoint — the same
address the rendered Service publishes and every consumer dials. The
entrypoint composes the migrate URL from it plus the clickhouse user and
password the Job already reads from the environment's Secret, so the address
has one source and no credential value is rendered into the manifests. A
deployment whose endpoint resolves no in-cluster instance is refused by name
rather than rendering a Job that cannot connect.

The Job is named after what it runs: the service name plus the first 12 hex
characters of a SHA-256 over its rendered pod template — the same naming the
postgres agent gives its bootstrap Job. A Job's pod template is immutable,
so a Job that kept one name could never carry a new image.

When the render pins the migration image by digest — always under restricted
output, which refuses any other image; under a local apply only when the
build captured a digest — a changed migration set is a new image, and the
render names a new Job, which runs. A changed address or credential reference
does the same. An unchanged render keeps the name, and the completed Job is
left alone. A local apply of a tag-only image keeps the name while the
service's version is unchanged, even if `migrations/` changed: bump the
version, or render digest-pinned, to run them again.

The Job carries no `ttlSecondsAfterFinished`: once complete it stays, so the
manifest that declares it stays in sync and its outcome stays inspectable
(`kubectl logs -l codefly.dev/bootstrap-service=<service>`). A reconciler that applies the rendered tree with
pruning removes the previous Job when a render names a new one.
